course: medicine
lesson: 54
title: A balanced plate for a kid who plays sport
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sporty kids don't need a special diet: the UK Eatwell Guide shows one plate for everybody, with about a third fruit and veg, just over a third starchy food like pasta, rice, potatoes and bread, and the rest protein, dairy and a little oil.
explain: Ollie squints at the coach's poster, «Where's the chocolate section?», but sweets aren't one of the main groups; they sit off the plate as an occasional extra.
explain: No single food has everything, so eating only pasta like Jade in Belfast misses whole groups, and «eat the rainbow» means peppers, carrots, berries and spinach, not Skittles.
explain: Sport burns energy but doesn't protect teeth: as Bella's coach in Brisbane says, «Your teeth don't go to training, mate», and a study reported that about 4 in 10 professional footballers it checked had tooth decay.
fact: The Eatwell Guide is the UK picture of a balanced diet, and it is the same for sporty and non-sporty children.
fact: In the Eatwell Guide about a third of the plate is fruit and veg and just over a third is starchy food, so together they make up most of it.
fact: The other parts are protein foods such as beans, eggs, fish and meat, dairy or alternatives, and small amounts of unsaturated oils; sweets and fizzy drinks are not one of the main groups.
fact: Starchy food is great fuel, but the body also needs protein, fruit and veg, dairy and some fats, because no single food has everything.
fact: UK advice is at least 5 portions of fruit and veg a day, and a child's portion is roughly what fits in their hand.
fact: Fruit and veg of different colours give different vitamins and minerals, so eating many colours gives a good mix.
fact: Exercise uses energy, but sugar still feeds the germs on teeth that cause decay, so being sporty doesn't protect teeth.
fact: UK advice for children aged 7 to 10 is no more than about 24 g of free sugars a day, about 6 sugar cubes.
fact: A study published in 2015 reported that about 4 in 10 players it checked at professional UK football clubs had tooth decay, and frequent sugary sports drinks, gels and bars were thought to be part of the reason.
fact: Each time sugar touches the teeth, acid attacks them for a while, so sweets drip-fed through training mean many attacks; with a meal in one go is kinder to teeth.
fact: Protein helps muscles grow and repair, and a child of 7 to 10 needs about 28 g a day, which ordinary meals easily give; extra isn't stored as muscle.
fact: A big chocolate bar before a match can give a quick lift then a slump and sit uneasily in the stomach; toast, a banana or cereal give steadier energy.
fact: Growing, sporty children need regular meals, and skipping meals leaves them tired, moody and slower and harms growth.
fact: In Wales «tidy» means good or great, and in Northern Ireland «wee» means small.
exercise: What is the Eatwell Guide? | The UK picture of a balanced plate | A list of foods meant for professional athletes only | A set menu from a famous restaurant in London | A school rule that bans all sweets and fizzy drinks
exercise: About how much of the Eatwell plate is fruit and veg? | About a third | About a tenth | All of it | None of it
exercise: Which two groups make up most of the plate together? | Fruit and veg, and starchy food | Sweets, cakes and fizzy drinks | Meat, fish, eggs and cheese | Butter, oil and spreads together
exercise: Where do chocolate and sweets fit? | Off the main plate, as occasional extras | As the biggest section, since sport needs sugar | As the protein section, next to meat and fish | As one of your 5 a day, like fruit is counted
exercise: Why not eat only pasta? | No single food has everything you need | Pasta has no energy in it at all, it is just filler | Pasta makes your bones soft | Footballers are banned from pasta
exercise: How many portions of fruit and veg a day at least? | 5 | 1 | 2 | 20
exercise: About how big is a child's portion of fruit or veg? | What fits in their hand | A whole bucket | One single pea | A full dinner plate
exercise: What does «eat the rainbow» mean? | Eat fruit and veg of many colours | Eat sweets of many colours as a reward after sport | Eat only red food | Eat food outdoors after rain
exercise: Does lots of sport stop sugar harming your teeth? | No, sugar still feeds the germs on teeth | Yes, running about scrubs the sugar off your teeth | Yes, sweat and fast breathing protect the teeth from sugar | Only for swimmers, because the pool water rinses them
exercise: What did a 2015 study report about pro footballers' teeth? | About 4 in 10 checked had tooth decay | None had any decay | All had perfect teeth | About 1 in 1,000 had decay
exercise: Why is snacking on sweets all through training worse for teeth? | Each snack starts a new acid attack | Sweets melt faster in your mouth when you run about | Teeth get tired during training | It isn't, spreading it out is best
exercise: About how much protein does a child of 7 to 10 need a day? | About 28 g, from ordinary meals | About 500 g, from a whole chicken | None at all | Only from protein shakes
exercise: Does eating extra protein turn into extra muscle? | No, extra isn't stored as muscle | Yes, every gram of it gets turned into new muscle | Yes, but only meat protein | Only if you eat it at night
exercise: Why is a family chocolate bar a poor pre-match snack? | It can give a lift, then a slump and a sick tummy | It has no energy in it, only colouring and air | It is too heavy to carry | It makes you too fast
exercise: What happens if a sporty child skips meals before a gala (a swimming race day)? | She gets tired, moody and slower | She gets lighter, faster and better at her strokes | She grows taller because her body uses up its fat | Nothing changes, as long as she drinks enough water
exercise: What does «tidy» mean in Wales? | Good or great | Messy | Tiny | Angry
test: Ollie at the Eatwell poster: «where's the chocolate section?» What's the truth? | Sweets aren't a main group, just an occasional extra | It's the biggest part of the plate | It's hidden in the fruit section | Sporty kids get a chocolate section
test: Jade: «footballers eat pasta, so I'll eat only pasta, sure». What would happen if she did for months? | She'd miss vitamins, protein and other things she needs | She'd become a pro footballer | Nothing, pasta has everything | She'd never be hungry again
test: Thomas: banana, apple, carrots, peas, strawberries. «Is that enough, Miss?» | Yes, that's 5 portions | No, he needs 50 | No, peas don't count | Yes, but only the fruit counts
test: Bella: «I play sport heaps, sugar doesn't count for me, I burn it off». Her coach is right because? | Sugar still harms teeth whatever sport you do | Sport makes sugar dangerous, so sporty kids must stop | Sugar only counts on Sundays | Bella doesn't play enough sport
test: Charlie: «pros with rotten teeth? no way». What did the study report? | About 4 in 10 checked had decay | Only goalkeepers had decay, because they stand still | None of them had any decay | Every single player had decay
test: Rory has a jelly sweet every 15 minutes in a 2-hour training: «just a few, innit». How many sugar attacks is that? | 8 | 2 | 15 | 120
test: Amara: «eat the rainbow, like Skittles?» What does her coach mean? | Peppers, carrots, berries and spinach | Any sweets, as long as they come in lots of colours | Food dyed with bright colouring to look like a rainbow | Only green vegetables, since green is the healthiest
test: Will: «I'm sporty, I need a whole chicken a day, tidy». His mam says a bit at each meal will do. Who's right? | His mam; normal meals give enough protein | Will; sporty kids need huge amounts of meat | Both; a chicken for breakfast only | Neither; kids need no protein
test: Leo eats a whole family chocolate bar «for energy» and feels sick on the pitch. What would be better? | Toast, a banana or cereal | Two smaller chocolate bars | A big bag of crisps | Nothing at all
test: Hannah won't eat tea to «save herself» for the gala and is tired and teary all week. What's true? | Skipping meals makes young athletes weaker and more tired | Eating less makes you a better swimmer | She'll swim faster on an empty tank | Tiredness is just nerves, nothing to do with food
test: Mum says «wee bit of everything, pet» and adds veg and chicken to Jade's pasta. Why is that good? | Different food groups give different things the body needs | Chicken makes pasta taste of nothing | Veg cools the pasta down | Mixing foods slows digestion forever
test: What would happen to a team that sipped sugary sports drinks and gels all through every training for years? | Their teeth would be at higher risk of decay | Their teeth would get stronger from all the extra sugar | Their teeth would turn white | Nothing would happen to their teeth
exam: Identify the publication which constitutes the official representation of a balanced diet in the United Kingdom. | The Eatwell Guide | The Athletes' Handbook | The Chocolate Charter | The Protein Register
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the proportion of the Eatwell Guide allocated to fruit and vegetables. | Approximately one third | Approximately one tenth | The entirety | No proportion whatsoever
exam: It is given that a child consumes a confection at intervals of fifteen minutes during a training session of two hours. Determine the number of occasions upon which the teeth are exposed to sugar. | Eight | Two | Fifteen | One hundred and twenty
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the protein requirements of a child engaged in sport? | Ordinary balanced meals provide sufficient protein | Supplementation with shakes is indispensable | A whole chicken is required daily | Protein is not required by children
